{"id":"730ba8b2-766e-4254-97f3-abac2ddafb5d","object":"chat.completion","created":1784983752,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"import json\\n\\ndef solve(case):\\n    if 'nodes' not in case or 'required_symbols' not in case:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing required fields\\\")\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list) or len(nodes) == 0:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"nodes must be non-empty list\\\")\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_map = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if not all(k in n for k in ('id','parent','symbol','depth','removable','utility')):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Node missing required fields\\\")\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid in node_map:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Duplicate node id: {nid}\\\")\\n        node_map[nid] = n\\n        parent = n.get('parent')\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        parent = n['parent']\\n        if parent is not None and parent not in node_map:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Node {n['id']} has unknown parent {parent}\\\")\\n    if 'root' not in node_map:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Root node missing\\\")\\n    if node_map['root']['parent'] is not None:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Root node must have null parent\\\")\\n    retained_ids = set(node_map.keys())\\n    removed_ids = []\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid == 'root':\\n            continue\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if n['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        child_ids = children.get(nid, [])\\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained_ids for cid in child_ids)\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        removed_ids.append(nid)\\n        retained_ids.remove(nid)\\n    sorted_retained = sorted(retained_ids)\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_ids,\\n        'retained_node_ids': sorted_retained\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write a `solve(case)` function that implements grammar-guided node reduction based on the description. The task is similar to Perses-style reduction: we have a tree of nodes with ids, parent, symbol, depth, removable flag, utility. We need to remove a subset of removable nodes while preserving that all required_symbols are still present in the retained tree. Also we must keep root (non-removable) and nodes that are non-removable? Actually removable flag indicates if it can be removed. The root is non-removable. Also nodes with required symbols? The description says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" Also \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we need to decide which removable nodes to actually remove. The objective is to minimize total utility loss? Not explicitly stated. But the candidate_artifact mentions \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" and \"bounded objective\". The public fixtures show a tree. The output requires removed_node_ids (list of ids in order of removal?) and retained_node_ids. The \"actual removal order\" suggests we need to track order of removal. We need to consider deeper nodes before shallower nodes. So process nodes in decreasing depth (deepest first). For each node, if it's removable and not root and not required symbol, we test if removing it (and its subtree?) would still leave all required symbols present somewhere in the tree. But we also need to consider that removing a node means its children are also removed (unless they are retained elsewhere? Actually if we remove a node, its entire subtree is removed. The tree is parent-child based. So we need to check if after removing that node and its descendants, all required_symbols still appear among retained nodes. Also we cannot remove a node if it has retained children? The description: \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" That suggests if we decide to keep some children, the parent must stay. So we must process bottom-up: first decide on leaves, then propagate up. Since we process deeper nodes first, we make decisions on children before parents. For each node, if it's removable and not required, we can consider removing it only if all required symbols are still present in the retained set after removal. But we also need to check that its children are not retained (i.e., all children are also removed or already removed). Actually \"node with retained children\" means if any child is retained (i.e., not removed), then the parent cannot be removed because it's needed to connect the tree. So in bottom-up, we first decide on children. If a child is retained, then the parent cannot be removed. So we can mark nodes as \"to be removed\" but only if all its descendants are also removed (or if we decide to remove them). Because the removal order: we remove nodes one by one. Typically, we remove leaf nodes first, then their parents become leaves, etc. But the algorithm: for each node in decreasing depth, if it's removable and not required, and if all required symbols are still present in the current tree (considering that we may have already removed some nodes), and if it has no retained children (i.e., all its children have been removed), then we can remove it. This is like a greedy reduction.\n\nWe also need to consider utility? The candidate_artifact mentions \"utility\" but no constrained objective like minimize utility loss. It says \"bounded objective\" but not specified. The output only requires removed and retained node ids. Possibly the goal is to remove as many removable nodes as possible while preserving required symbols? Or maybe we need to minimize total utility of removed nodes? The description: \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" The interestingness oracle here is represented by required_symbols being present. So we want to reduce the tree (remove nodes) while ensuring all required symbols are still present.\n\nGiven the public fixtures, the expected output is not given, but we can infer from the problem statement that we need to produce a list of removed node ids in order of removal, and retained node ids. We need to implement a deterministic algorithm.\n\nLet's design algorithm:\n\n1. Build a map from node id to node object.\n2. Build a mapping from parent id to list of child ids.\n3. Build a set of required symbols.\n4. Initialize a set of removed_ids = empty list.\n5. Initialize a set of retained_ids = set of all node ids initially? Actually we will start with all nodes present, then remove some. At the end, retained are those not removed.\n6. Process nodes in order of decreasing depth (ties: use node id as stable tie break). For each node:\n   - if node.id == 'root' or not node.removable or node.symbol in required_symbols: skip (cannot remove).\n   - Check if node has any retained children (i.e., any child id that is not in removed_ids). If yes, skip (cannot remove parent before children are removed).\n   - Check if removing this node (and its subtree) would cause any required symbol to become missing. That means: after removal, the retained tree must still contain at least one node for each required symbol. Since we are only removing this node and its descendants (but we already removed its children if any, because we process bottom-up, so children are either removed or retained; if children are retained, we already skipped). So the only nodes removed at this point are this node itself and any already removed descendants. But we need to ensure that for each required symbol, there exists at least one node with that symbol outside of the subtree of this node (or within other branches that are not removed). So we can compute the count of each required symbol in the whole tree initially (or dynamically). Simpler: maintain a counter of how many nodes of each required symbol remain in the retained set (excluding nodes that are already removed). Initially, count total nodes per symbol. When we consider removing a node, we need to subtract its symbol contribution and the contribution of all its descendants that are not yet removed (but we only remove descendants that have already been removed? Actually if we process bottom-up, all descendants of this node that are removable and not required would have been already processed and possibly removed. But those that are non-removable or required would still be present. So the subtree under this node consists of: the node itself, plus some nodes that are already removed (if they were removable and not required and processed earlier), plus nodes that are non-removable or required. So when we remove this node, we also remove its entire subtree including those non-removable/required descendants? That would violate the required symbols constraint. So we cannot remove a node if any of its descendants are required symbol nodes (non-removable) or are non-removable nodes that are required for structure? The description says \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" Retained children meaning children we decided to keep. So if any descendant is a required symbol node, we must keep that descendant, and thus we cannot remove the ancestor. Because removing the ancestor would remove that descendant as well. So we must ensure that all descendants of a node we consider removing are already removed (i.e., we have previously decided to remove them). So we need to check that the entire subtree of this node (excluding node itself) consists only of nodes that are already in removed_ids. If any descendant is still present (i.e., not removed), then we cannot remove this node. This is a stronger condition: we can only remove a leaf (in the current remaining tree). So algorithm:\n   - For each node in decreasing depth, if removable and not root and symbol not required:\n        - Check if all children (direct children) are already removed. If any child is not removed, skip.\n        - But also deeper descendants? Actually if all direct children are removed, then all descendants must be removed because children are removed only if their subtrees were fully removed. So we only need to check direct children.\n        - Then compute the required symbols count in the retained set after removal. Since the subtree being removed contains only already removed nodes plus this node, the subtraction of required symbol counts is just the symbol of this node. Because already removed nodes are already subtracted. So we need to ensure that the current count of each required symbol (including this node if it has a required symbol, but it doesn't because we skip required symbols) minus 0 (since its symbol is not required) remains >= 1. So no issue.\n        - But what if a required symbol appears only in this node's subtree? Then we must have kept that node, but we already ensure that required symbol nodes are not removable and we never attempt to remove them. So the subtree must contain at least one required symbol node that is non-removable, which would have prevented us from removing the parent because that child (or descendant) is retained. So the check \"all children are removed\" already prevents that because the required symbol node would be a retained child. So fine.\n   - If conditions satisfied, remove the node: add its id to removed_ids list (in order of removal).\n   - Continue.\n\nAfter processing all nodes, retained_ids = all node ids minus removed_ids. Sort retained_ids? The output schema for retained_node_ids is array of strings. The description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So we should sort retained_node_ids alphabetically? The schema doesn't specify order, but \"sorted\" implies lexicographically. So we'll sort. For removed_node_ids, we keep the order of removal (the order we processed and removed).\n\nTesting with the given fixtures: The first payload has required_symbols: [\"root\",\"s1\"]. root is present. s1 appears in n1 (depth1) and n4 (depth2). n1 is non-removable, n4 is removable. n2 is removable. n3 is non-removable. n4 parent is n2. n6 is removable parent n1. Process depths: depth2 nodes: n4, n5, n6. n4: removable? Yes, symbol s1? Actually n4 symbol s1, which is required. So we cannot remove n4 because required symbol. n5: symbol s2, removable false. n6: symbol s0, removable true. Process n6: check children: none (leaf). Its symbol not required. Check if all children removed (true). Remove n6. removed_ids = [\"n6\"]. Then process n4: it has children? none. But symbol required => skip. n5: non-removable skip. Then depth1 nodes: n1 (non-removable, required symbol s1) skip. n2: removable, symbol s2 not required. Children: n4 (still present because not removed), n4 is retained child? n4 is not removed. So cannot remove n2 because child n4 is retained. n3: non-removable. So only n6 removed. Then root non-removable. So removed_node_ids = [\"n6\"], retained = all except n6: sorted list of [\"root\",\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\"]. That seems plausible.\n\nSecond fixture similar.\n\nThird fixture similar.\n\nBut we need to ensure the algorithm matches the \"grammar_valid\" and other properties. The description says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So when processing nodes in decreasing depth, within same depth, sort by node id ascending? That's a stable tie break. We'll sort nodes by depth descending, then by id ascending.\n\nAlso we must \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" Already accounted.\n\nAll required fields present: nodes list with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. required_symbols list. We'll validate.\n\nReturn removed_node_ids (order of removal) and retained_node_ids (sorted).\n\nEdge cases: If a required symbol appears only in a node that is removable, we cannot remove it. Also if a removable node has a child that is required but removable, we cannot remove the parent until that child is removed, but the child cannot be removed because it's required. So parent stays. That's fine.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # case is dict with 'nodes' and 'required_symbols'\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    # Build maps\n    node_map = {}\n    children = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        nid = n['id']\n        node_map[nid] = n\n        parent = n.get('parent')\n        if parent is not None:\n            children.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\n    # Ensure root present and parent null\n    # Initialize removed set and order list\n    removed_ids = []\n    retained_ids = set(node_map.keys())\n    \n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    \n    for n in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = n['id']\n        # Cannot remove root\n        if nid == 'root':\n            continue\n        # Must be removable\n        if not n['removable']:\n            continue\n        # Must not be a required symbol\n        if n['symbol'] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        # Check if all direct children are already removed (i.e., not in retained_ids)\n        child_ids = children.get(nid, [])\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained_ids for cid in child_ids)\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # Now removal is possible. Check required symbols still present after removal.\n        # Since we only remove this node, we need to ensure that for each required symbol, there is at least one node with that symbol still in retained_ids after removal.\n        # We can compute a quick check: if this node's symbol is required (already skipped), so no issue. But what if a required symbol appears only in the subtree of this node? Since we already ensured no retained children, the only nodes in the subtree are those already removed and this node. So if a required symbol existed in the subtree, it would have been either in a non-removable node which would be a retained child, or in a removable required node which we would not have removed. So not possible. However, we can do a safe check: \n        # Compute remaining counts after removal (temporarily remove nid from retained_ids, check).\n        # But that might be expensive. Simpler: since we only remove leaf nodes (no retained children), and the node's symbol not required, the required symbols set is unchanged. So we can proceed.\n        # Actually consider scenario: required symbol appears only in a node that is a descendant of this node but that descendant is non-removable and thus a retained child. That would cause has_retained_child to be true, so we skip. So safe.\n        # Therefore we can remove.\n        removed_ids.append(nid)\n        retained_ids.remove(nid)\n    \n    # After loop, retained_ids is set of ids not removed\n    # Sort retained_ids alphabetically\n    sorted_retained = sorted(retained_ids)\n    \n    return {\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_ids,\n        'retained_node_ids': sorted_retained\n    }\n\nBut we must also ensure we don't mutate input; we only read.\n\nThe output must be JSON-serializable. The function should return a dict matching schema.\n\nAlso need to raise ValueError for infeasible or structurally invalid input? The description says \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it.\" So we should validate that the root exists, that all nodes have required fields, that parents exist, etc. But the fixtures seem valid. We'll add basic validation.\n\nNow we need to produce the final response: a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value being the Python source code as a string. Must escape newlines, backslashes, quotes.\n\nWe'll write the Python code and then encode as JSON string.\n\nI'll produce the code carefully.\n\nLet's write:\n\nimport json\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # Validate case structure\n    if 'nodes' not in case or 'required_symbols' not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"Missing required fields\")\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list) or len(nodes) == 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"nodes must be non-empty list\")\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    # Build maps and validate\n    node_map = {}\n    children = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        if not all(k in n for k in ('id','parent','symbol','depth','removable','utility')):\n            raise ValueError(\"Node missing required fields\")\n        nid = n['id']\n        if nid in node_map:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Duplicate node id: {nid}\")\n        node_map[nid] = n\n        parent = n.get('parent')  # None for root\n        if parent is not None:\n            if parent not in node_map and parent != 'root': # parent might not have been seen yet? Actually we check after building full map? Need to validate later.\n            # Better: store children and then after full map, validate parent existence\n            children.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\n    # After loop, validate parent references\n    for n in nodes:\n        parent = n['parent']\n        if parent is not None and parent not in node_map:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Node {n['id']} has unknown parent {parent}\")\n    # Ensure root exists\n    if 'root' not in node_map:\n        raise ValueError(\"Root node missing\")\n    # Ensure root parent is None\n    if node_map['root']['parent'] is not None:\n        raise ValueError(\"Root node must have null parent\")\n    \n    # Initial retained set\n    retained_ids = set(node_map.keys())\n    removed_ids = []  # ordered list\n    \n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending (stable tie)\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    \n    for n in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = n['id']\n        # Never remove root\n        if nid == 'root':\n            continue\n        # Must be removable\n        if not n['removable']:\n            continue\n        # Must not have required symbol\n        if n['symbol'] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        # Check if any direct child is still retained\n        child_ids = children.get(nid, [])\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained_ids for cid in child_ids)\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # Check that removing this node does not eliminate any required symbol\n        # Since we only remove nodes that are not required and have no retained children,\n        # the only way a required symbol could be lost is if it existed only in this node's subtree and\n        # all retained nodes in that subtree are already removed. But if a required symbol existed\n        # in a non-removable node, that node would be a retained child, so we skip.\n        # For safety, we can still test explicitly:\n        # For each required symbol, ensure there is at least one node with that symbol\n        # that will remain after removal. We'll compute a quick count.\n        # We'll temporarily remove nid from retained_ids, check, then add back if failing.\n        # But to avoid performance issues, we do a simple check: the only potential loss is if\n        # this node itself has a required symbol (already excluded) or if its subtree (excluding already removed)\n        # contains the only occurrence. But we already checked no retained children, so subtree has no retained nodes.\n        # So safe.\n        removed_ids.append(nid)\n        retained_ids.remove(nid)\n    \n    # Sort retained IDs alphabetically\n    sorted_retained = sorted(retained_ids)\n    \n    return {\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_ids,\n        'retained_node_ids': sorted_retained\n    }\n\nBut we need to be careful: children dict may have nodes that are not yet processed? The sorting ensures we process in order. The children dict is built from all nodes.\n\nAlso we need to consider that when we remove a node, we don't need to update children of its parent because the parent check only uses children list from static mapping. That's fine: we check if any child of node is still retained; we don't consider that node is removed so child is removed from retained set. So the parent's child removal is implicit because we only check retained_ids.\n\nNow we need to ensure that the algorithm correctly handles cases where a node has descendants that are not directly its children but are already removed, and the node's direct children may be all removed, but there might be a non-removable descendant that is not a direct child? Actually if all direct children are removed, then all deeper descendants are also removed because removal is bottom-up and a child is removed only if its own children are all removed. So the condition is sufficient.\n\nBut there is a subtlety: What if a removable node has a child that is also removable, but that child is not removed because it has a retained child? Then the parent cannot be removed because child is retained. That's correct.\n\nSo the algorithm seems correct.\n\nNow we need to produce the final JSON. I'll write the Python code as a string, properly escaped.\n\nI'll use triple-quoted string inside JSON? No, I need to embed the source code as a JSON string value. So I'll write the code, then replace newlines with \\n, backslashes with \\\\, quotes with \\\". I'll do that manually or via Python's json.dumps. But the output must be a JSON object with one key. I can use Python to generate it, but here I'm writing the answer manually. I'll carefully format.\n\nLet me produce the code as a single line with \\n. I'll ensure proper indentation with spaces.\n\nI'll include a main guard? Not needed.\n\nI'll also comment that the function uses only standard library.\n\nI'll write:\n\n\"implementation\": \"import json\\n\\ndef solve(case):\\n    if 'nodes' not in case or 'required_symbols' not in case:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing required fields\\\")\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list) or len(nodes) == 0:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"nodes must be non-empty list\\\")\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_map = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if not all(k in n for k in ('id','parent','symbol','depth','removable','utility')):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Node missing required fields\\\")\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid in node_map:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Duplicate node id: {nid}\\\")\\n        node_map[nid] = n\\n        parent = n.get('parent')\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        parent = n['parent']\\n        if parent is not None and parent not in node_map:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Node {n['id']} has unknown parent {parent}\\\")\\n    if 'root' not in node_map:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Root node missing\\\")\\n    if node_map['root']['parent'] is not None:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Root node must have null parent\\\")\\n    retained_ids = set(node_map.keys())\\n    removed_ids = []\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid == 'root':\\n            continue\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if n['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        child_ids = children.get(nid, [])\\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained_ids for cid in child_ids)\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n           